add("colorado-rule-29-exclusion-list-bettors-threaten-athletes-harassment",
"Colorado Rule 29: Excluding Bettors Who Threaten Athletes","Regulatory",
"Colorado's Division of Gaming wants threats against athletes to justify exclusion. What sportsbooks should do before the vote.",
["united-states"],["fox-rothschild-llp","greenberg-traurig-llp","ifrah-law-pllc","duane-morris-llp"],"2026-11-14T07:30:00Z",[
"The Colorado Division of Gaming has opened rulemaking to amend Rule 29, the provision that governs the state's Involuntary Exclusion List, so that threatening or harassing athletes becomes an express ground for barring a person from licensed gaming. A rulemaking workshop was held on 12 November 2026, and the Division intends to take the proposal to the Limited Gaming Control Commission. A formal hearing cannot take place until at least 30 days after notice is filed, and approved changes would be expected to take effect roughly 45 to 60 days after the Commission votes, which points to an effective date in the first half of 2027.",
"The substance of the amendment is an expansion of the factors the Commission already weighs when deciding whether a person should be placed on the list. The draft adds threats of physical harm against athletes and their immediate families, against officials of sports governing bodies, teams and coaching staff and their families, against staff or patrons of licensed casinos and sportsbooks, and against Division staff. Convictions for crimes in which those groups were victims would also be relevant, as would betting in breach of Colorado's insider betting restrictions in section 44-30-1502. The reputation factor is broadened to capture anyone who has manipulated or compromised the integrity of gaming or sports betting.",
"Alongside the rule, the Division has launched an online harassment reporting form that allows athletes and others to submit names, roles, sports, leagues, incident descriptions and supporting documents. The Division says it can investigate reports and coordinate with local law enforcement. Regulators have been candid that the form is designed to tackle the problem leagues describe as the biggest obstacle, which is under-reporting. That framing matters legally: the quality of the evidence behind any exclusion decision will depend heavily on what athletes, teams and operators feed into the process.",
"There is an important limit that operators should understand. As the Division itself acknowledged, it cannot currently compel online sportsbooks to ban individuals placed on the Involuntary Exclusion List from their platforms; staff have said the agency strongly recommends that operators follow the list voluntarily. In practice, few licensees will want to be the platform that continues to take bets from a person the state has publicly identified as a threat to players. Voluntary compliance therefore carries real reputational weight, and it would be unsurprising if a future legislative session converted the recommendation into a binding obligation.",
"Colorado is joining a growing group of states. Louisiana, Ohio and West Virginia already have measures aimed at bettors who abuse participants, Massachusetts runs a comparable programme, and the NCAA has recently joined an industry bad-actor initiative focused on online abuse of college athletes. In September, professional leagues and players' associations jointly asked state regulators to adopt stricter standards. The result is an emerging patchwork in which the same individual may be excluded in one state, flagged in another and unrestricted in a third, which creates both compliance complexity and an opportunity for multi-state operators to adopt a single, higher internal standard.",
"From a due-process perspective, involuntary exclusion is a significant sanction, and placement decisions will need to rest on reliable evidence and fair procedure. Online abuse is often anonymous or pseudonymous, and linking a social media account to a verified betting customer raises questions of data sharing, privacy and evidential standards that the draft does not fully answer. Operators that share customer information with the Division in support of an exclusion should ensure they have a lawful basis to do so under their privacy notices and any applicable state privacy law, and should document the matching process.",
"We recommend that Colorado licensees use the comment period to press for clarity on three points: how exclusion information will be transmitted to online platforms, what standard of proof the Commission will apply, and whether operators acting in good faith on the list will be protected from customer claims. Internally, sportsbooks should review their terms of service to confirm they can close accounts for abusive conduct toward participants, align their integrity and responsible gambling teams on escalation procedures, and prepare to monitor the list as it grows."])

add("isle-of-man-gsc-aml-cft-guidance-v1-4-b2b-group-structures-risk",
"Isle of Man GSC AML Guidance v1.4: What Changes","Compliance",
"The GSC's updated AML/CFT guidance widens the risk-based approach to B2B, group and ownership risk. Operators should review frameworks now.",
["isle-of-man","united-kingdom"],["cains","m-and-p-legal","mannbenham-advocates","kosnahan-law"],"2026-11-14T08:45:00Z",[
"The Isle of Man Gambling Supervision Commission has published version 1.4 of its anti-money laundering and countering the financing of terrorism guidance for gambling operators, released on 10 November 2026. The update follows the island's 2025 and 2026 national risk assessments and the sector-specific money laundering risk assessment published earlier this year. The Commission has been explicit that the law itself has not changed: the Gambling (Anti-Money Laundering and Countering the Financing of Terrorism) Code 2019 remains the operative instrument. What has changed is the Commission's articulation of how it expects licence holders to apply the risk-based approach in practice, and that is often where supervisory findings are made.",
"The most significant development is the widening of the risk lens beyond the individual player. The guidance now expects operators to assess risk arising from their wider business relationships, including B2B arrangements, group structures and customer and beneficial ownership risk. For an island whose licensed community is weighted towards B2B suppliers, platform providers and remote operators running multi-entity groups, this is not a marginal change. It requires compliance teams to evidence that they understand who they are doing business with at the corporate level, and how those relationships could be exploited.",
"The refreshed sections are broad. They cover business, customer and technology risk assessments; identification and verification; customer due diligence and enhanced due diligence; payments and transactions; terrorist and proliferation financing; financial sanctions; internal and external suspicious activity reporting; and staff training and appointments. The Commission has also clarified its supervisory expectations, updated references to national and international guidance and refreshed the explanatory diagrams. In our experience, refreshed diagrams and worked expectations are frequently what examiners use as the benchmark during inspections, so operators should read them closely.",
"The threat picture explains the emphasis. The February 2026 sector assessment rated gambling overall, and online gambling specifically, as medium-high risk, with land-based gambling rated medium-low. Among the principal threats identified were criminal ownership of gambling or B2B businesses through front companies and complex corporate structures, and the activity of organised crime groups, including groups linked to East and Southeast Asia. Those findings translate directly into the guidance's focus on ownership transparency, group-level risk and the integrity of commercial counterparties.",
"For B2B licence holders, the practical consequence is that counterparty due diligence must be more than a sanctions screen and a certificate of incorporation. Suppliers should be able to show how they assess the operators they serve, including licensing status, ultimate beneficial owners, the markets in which their content is made available and the controls in place to prevent distribution into unlicensed jurisdictions. This aligns with the direction of travel in the United Kingdom and the Netherlands, where regulators have increasingly treated suppliers as gatekeepers rather than passive technology vendors.",
"The Commission has asked licence holders to review the new guidance, assess whether their AML, CFT and counter-proliferation financing frameworks need amendment, and make any necessary changes as soon as practicable. It has also offered drop-in sessions at its offices. The absence of a fixed deadline should not be read as leniency. A licence holder inspected in 2027 will be expected to show that it considered version 1.4, documented any gap analysis and either updated its policies or recorded why no change was needed. The Commission is also revising separate guidance for software suppliers, network services permission holders and virtual assets and goods, and has invited industry input.",
"We recommend a structured gap analysis against each refreshed section, with particular attention to group-wide risk assessments, beneficial ownership verification across corporate chains and the documentation of B2B counterparty risk. The results should be reported to the board, and the MLRO should hold a record of decisions taken. Licence holders with network services permissions or virtual asset exposure should engage with the forthcoming consultations now, because the detail of those guidance notes will shape what the Commission expects in the next supervisory cycle."])

add("brazil-spa-normative-instruction-32-2026-financial-reserve-bettor-refunds-mp-1394",
"Brazil SPA IN 32/2026: Releasing the R$5m Betting Reserve","Licensing",
"Brazil's SPA has set the rules for using and releasing operators' R$5 million reserve after MP 1.394. Bettor refunds come first.",
["brazil"],["pinheiro-neto-advogados","mattos-filho"],"2026-11-14T10:00:00Z",[
"Brazil's Secretariat of Prizes and Bets has issued Normative Instruction SPA/MF No. 32, dated 6 November 2026 and published in an extra edition of the Diário Oficial da União on 10 November, setting the procedure for using and releasing the minimum R$5 million financial reserve that every authorised fixed-odds operator was required to maintain under article 9 of Portaria Normativa SPA/MF No. 615/2024. The instruction is the latest piece of the wind-down architecture created by Provisional Measure No. 1,394/2026, which banned betting in the country, and it took effect on publication.",
"The reserve, held in federal public bonds and segregated from operating accounts, was designed as insolvency protection for bettors. The instruction preserves that purpose. Any movement of the reserve without prior SPA authorisation remains prohibited, and only two uses are permitted: paying bettors where the funds in transactional accounts are insufficient to return all amounts owed, and returning the reserve to the company once no known obligations to bettors remain. The reserve may not be used to cover operating, administrative, labour, tax or corporate costs, or the expenses of winding down the business.",
"Use of the reserve to pay bettors is a last resort. An operator may apply only after completing the measures required by article 8 of the provisional measure, and must declare that all other sources of funding have been exhausted. The application must include an individual list of the bettors to be paid, with a requested amount equal to the total owed. The SPA will reconcile the request against data submitted by banks and payment institutions through the Sigap system, and discrepancies must be explained. Once approved, the custodian sells the bonds and transfers the proceeds to an account designated by Caixa Econômica Federal, which makes the refunds directly.",
"The declaration required from administrators deserves careful attention. Annex II requires them to acknowledge that a shortfall in transactional accounts is potentially incompatible with article 4, paragraphs 3 and 7, of Portaria 615, which govern the segregation and sufficiency of player funds. Authorisation to use the reserve does not amount to a finding that account management was proper and does not preclude later investigation. Signatories are personally liable administratively, civilly and criminally for false, inaccurate or incomplete information, and they cannot sign through a proxy. In effect, the application is also an admission that may be used in subsequent proceedings.",
"Recovering the reserve is a more demanding process. The company must file a request form together with financial statements for 2025 and for a position at 31 October 2026 or later, audited by an independent auditor registered with the CVM, as well as a special assurance report from the auditor concluding whether any obligations to bettors remain pending, and a further administrators' declaration that no balances, prizes, withdrawals or amounts from annulled bets are owed. If the SPA approves, the bonds are sold and the proceeds returned free of Portaria 615 restrictions, but return does not validate past operations and liability may still arise if obligations later surface.",
"The instruction sets no deadline for the SPA to decide on either type of request. Combined with the requirement for audited accounts at a date after 31 October, that means operators should not expect to recover reserves quickly. The direct withdrawal window for bettors closed on 5 October, so the population of unpaid customers should now be largely fixed, but the reconciliation against Sigap data is likely to expose differences between operators' internal records and banking data that will need to be resolved before any release.",
"Former licensees should treat the reserve process as a formal regulatory proceeding rather than an administrative formality. We recommend commissioning the auditor's assurance work early, reconciling player liabilities against payment institution records before filing, and obtaining legal review of every administrators' declaration given the personal criminal exposure attached to it. Operators pursuing compensation claims or constitutional challenges to the ban, including those linked to the proceedings before the Supreme Federal Court, should also consider how statements made in reserve applications might be relied upon against them in that litigation."])

add("italy-budget-bill-2027-article-25-lan-esports-venues-adm-entertainment-tax",
"Italy Budget Bill: ADM Oversight and Tax for LAN Venues","Tax",
"Article 25 of Italy's draft 2027 budget would bring LAN and esports venues under ADM, with authorisation fees and an 8% entertainment tax.",
["italy"],["cms-italy","dla-piper-italy","studio-legale-sbordoni-and-partners","norton-rose-fulbright-italy"],"2026-11-14T11:20:00Z",[
"Italy's draft 2027 budget law contains a provision that would extend the reach of the Customs and Monopolies Agency well beyond its traditional gambling perimeter. Article 25 of the bill would amend Article 110 of the Consolidated Law on Public Security (TULPS) to bring LAN gaming venues, meaning premises offering pure-skill video games without cash prizes on consoles, simulators and PCs, under ADM supervision. The video game industry association IIDEA published its objections on 10 November 2026 and is calling for the article to be deleted in full. The draft remains before Parliament and may change.",
"The proposed regime is a full authorisation model. Venues would require an ADM authorisation valid for two years and would need at least 20 gaming stations. Upfront payments are set at €200 per device, €1,000 per venue, €2,000 per game and €10,000 per online platform. Games would need a PEGI rating or equivalent, minors could enter but use only age-appropriate titles, and platforms would have to keep records of online activity for at least five years. ADM would set tournament rules, including participation fees and maximum prize values, and could levy a supervisory contribution of up to 8 per cent of participation fees.",
"The tax design is the most consequential element. An 8 per cent entertainment tax would apply to a flat taxable base of €15,000 per device or station, rising to €25,000 for large simulators, producing an annual charge of €1,200 per standard station. A venue at the minimum 20 stations would therefore owe €24,000 a year before any authorisation fees. Because the base is deemed rather than tied to actual revenue, the burden is fixed regardless of footfall, which disproportionately affects smaller venues and those outside major cities.",
"The government's own technical report acknowledges that reliable data on LAN venues does not exist. It projects 200 venues in 2027 generating around €5.87 million, and assumes 2,300 venues and about 46,000 stations from 2028, yielding roughly €55.2 million a year. IIDEA disputes those assumptions, estimating that only around 70 LAN venues operate in Italy, of which about 20 meet the 20-station threshold, and valuing the national esports sector at €32 to €35 million in 2026. On those figures, the projected tax take would exceed the size of the market it is meant to tax.",
"From a legal standpoint, the measure raises a classification question that matters beyond esports. Article 110 TULPS is the backbone of Italy's regulation of gaming devices, including AWP and VLT machines. Placing pure-skill games without cash prizes within the same framework blurs the line between regulated public gaming and ordinary entertainment, and could be cited in future debates about loot boxes, skill-based prize competitions and social casino products. Introducing a structural regulatory regime through a budget law, without a separate legislative process or sector consultation, may also invite challenge on grounds of proportionality and legislative competence.",
"Transitional rules would give existing venues 60 days to file a self-declaration, allowing them to continue operating while ADM adopts implementing measures, provided they pay the tax. The draft does not authorise betting or cash prizes on venue premises, so operators that host esports tournaments alongside betting-licensed businesses will need to keep those activities clearly separated. Licensed gambling operators with esports betting products should also follow the debate, since ADM's tournament rules could indirectly affect the integrity and data frameworks around events on which bets are offered.",
"Businesses potentially within scope, including venue operators, tournament organisers, publishers and online platforms, should engage now while the bill is in Parliament. Amendment proposals are most effective before the committee stage closes. In parallel, we recommend that affected businesses map their device counts, revenue models and platform activity against the draft definitions to quantify exposure, and prepare a self-declaration strategy in case Article 25 survives in its current form when the budget is approved at year end."])

add("european-regulators-halle-roundtable-illegal-gambling-portal-ip-blocking-prediction-markets",
"Halle Regulators Roundtable: Joint Illegal Gambling Portal","Enforcement",
"European regulators met in Halle to discuss a shared illegal-site portal, IP blocking and prediction markets. What it means for enforcement.",
["germany","spain","united-kingdom","italy","austria"],["hambach-and-hambach","redeker-sellner-dahs","cms-germany","harris-hagan"],"2026-11-14T12:40:00Z",[
"European gambling regulators gathered this month in Halle, home of Germany's Joint Gambling Authority, for the European Regulators Roundtable. Spain's Directorate General for the Regulation of Gambling, which was represented by its deputy directors for regulation and inspection, has published an account of the meeting. No formal agreements were announced, but the agenda shows clearly where cross-border enforcement against unlicensed operators is heading: shared intelligence on illegal sites, more effective blocking, a common view on prediction markets and continued scrutiny of advertising.",
"The most concrete proposal came from the United Kingdom, which suggested a joint information portal through which regulators could exchange up-to-date data on websites and apps identified as illegal. It remains a proposal rather than an adopted initiative. If implemented, however, it would build on the joint statement of November 2025, in which regulators from Austria, France, Great Britain, Italy, Portugal and Spain, supported by Germany, committed to share information on illegal operators and press digital platforms to block their advertising. A shared database would shorten the time between an unlicensed brand being identified in one market and action being taken in others.",
"A dedicated session examined IP address blocking, including results achieved, technical and operational difficulties and how international cooperation might improve effectiveness. Blocking has a mixed record. The UK Gambling Commission has warned that IP blocking can be circumvented and has relied heavily on search engine referrals, while in Germany the courts have constrained the GGL's ability to direct blocking orders at internet access providers, prompting legislative change to allow intermediaries to be targeted. Coordinated blocking, combined with payment blocking, is likely to be the focus of the next phase.",
"Prediction markets were the subject of a working group session in which each jurisdiction described its current position and how such platforms should be assessed under its framework. This is a live issue. As event contracts have grown in the United States and begun to target European users, regulators must decide whether they are financial instruments, betting products or both. A converging European view that sports and event contracts offered to consumers are gambling would make it harder for platforms to rely on financial services passporting, and would expose unlicensed offerings to the full range of gambling enforcement tools.",
"The DGOJ also presented the main findings of its impact analysis of Royal Decree 958/2020 on commercial communications, Spain's restrictive advertising regime. Other sessions covered anti-money laundering, public awareness and regulatory developments across participating jurisdictions in the second half of 2026. For licensed operators, the significance is that regulators are comparing notes on the effectiveness of advertising restrictions, which may influence the design of future rules in markets that are currently reviewing their frameworks, including Austria and Germany.",
"For unlicensed operators and their suppliers, the trend is unfavourable. Information shared through a common portal would make it easier for regulators to attribute brands across jurisdictions, to identify the B2B providers and payment intermediaries supporting them, and to coordinate sanctions. Suppliers that serve both licensed and unlicensed markets should assume that a finding in one jurisdiction will reach regulators in others, and that licence applications and renewals will increasingly be assessed against that shared record.",
"Licensed operators should see opportunity as well as risk. Better coordinated enforcement improves channelisation and the commercial case for compliance. We recommend that operators review their affiliate and media buying controls to ensure their brands cannot be confused with illegal offerings, that suppliers strengthen distribution monitoring and contractual termination rights, and that businesses considering prediction market products in Europe obtain jurisdiction-by-jurisdiction advice before launch rather than relying on a financial services characterisation that regulators are actively questioning."])

